If an interviewer asks “What is CDN, what is CloudFront, and how do they work?”, you can answer like this:
CDN stands for Content Delivery Network. It is a network of geographically distributed servers, called Edge Locations,
that cache and deliver content closer to users.

CloudFront is AWS's CDN service. It uses and manages AWS's global Edge Locations to provide CDN functionality.

When a user requests content, the request goes to the nearest CloudFront Edge Location. If the content is already cached there, 
CloudFront delivers it directly to the user. If it is not cached, CloudFront gets the content from the origin server, 
returns it to the user, and can cache a copy at the Edge Location for future requests.

CDN — Content Delivery Network

A CDN is a service that delivers your website/application content to users from a server that is geographically close to them.

In AWS, the main CDN service is Amazon CloudFront.

Simple example

Suppose your application server is in Mumbai:

              Your EC2
            Mumbai Region
                 |
                 |
        +--------+--------+
        |                 |
     User India       User USA

If a user in the USA requests an image:

USA User
   |
   | Request image
   ↓
EC2 in Mumbai
   |
   | Image
   ↓
USA User

The image has to travel from Mumbai to the USA, which can be slower.

With CDN
                 EC2 / S3
                Mumbai
                   |
                   ↓
              CloudFront
             /     |      \
            /      |       \
     India Edge  USA Edge  Europe Edge
        |           |          |
     User India  User USA  User Europe

CloudFront has edge locations around the world.

If the USA user requests an image:

USA User
   |
   ↓
CloudFront USA Edge
   |
   ↓
EC2/S3 in Mumbai       ← only if content isn't cached

If the image is already cached at the USA edge:

USA User
   |
   ↓
CloudFront USA Edge
   |
   ↓
Image

So the request doesn't need to go to Mumbai every time.

What does CDN usually cache?

Mostly static content:

Images
Videos
CSS
JavaScript
HTML
PDFs
Downloads
Why do we use CDN?

1. Faster response
Content comes from a nearby edge location.

2. Reduces load on EC2
CloudFront serves cached content instead of EC2 serving every request.

3. Reduces bandwidth usage
Fewer requests need to reach your origin server.

4. Global users get better performance
Users in different countries can access content from nearby edge locations.

Interview answer

CDN is a geographically distributed network of edge locations used to deliver content to users from a location close to them, reducing latency and improving application performance. In AWS, CloudFront is the CDN service.

Important: CDN is not a replacement for EC2. EC2 can be your origin server, while CloudFront sits in front of it and caches/delivers content.





