Route 53 in AWS

Amazon Route 53 is AWS's DNS (Domain Name System) service.
Route 53 is a global AWS service.
Simple meaning

Route 53 converts a domain name into the destination where your application is running.

For example:

User
  ↓
www.myapp.com
  ↓
Route 53
  ↓
Load Balancer
  ↓
EC2 instances

The user doesn't need to remember an IP address like:

13.234.56.78

They simply enter:

www.myapp.com

Route 53 finds where www.myapp.com should go.

Real-world example

Suppose your application has:

Domain:
www.amazon-example.com

Load Balancer:
my-alb-123456.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com

You configure Route 53:

www.amazon-example.com
        ↓
Route 53
        ↓
ALB
        ↓
EC2

When the user enters the domain:

User enters www.amazon-example.com
DNS lookup happens.
Route 53 receives the DNS query.
Route 53 returns the configured destination.
The user's request reaches the Load Balancer.
Load Balancer sends it to the appropriate EC2 instance.
Important distinction

Route 53 is not a Load Balancer.

Route 53
   ↓
Finds where the application is
   ↓
Load Balancer
   ↓
Distributes traffic
   ↓
EC2

So for an interview:
Route 53 is AWS's highly available and scalable DNS service. It translates domain names into destinations such as load balancers,
EC2 instances, or other AWS resources, and it can also perform DNS-based routing and health checks.


If you mean how Route 53 health checks work, think of it like this:

              Route 53
                 |
        Checks server health
          /             \
       Healthy        Unhealthy
          |                |
       Send user       Don't send user
Example

Suppose you have two servers:

www.myapp.com
      ↓
  Route 53
   /      \
EC2-A     EC2-B
Healthy   Healthy

Route 53 can periodically check whether an endpoint is working.

For example:

Route 53 → EC2-A
          "Are you healthy?"
             ↓
          HTTP 200 OK
             ↓
          HEALTHY ✅

If EC2-A stops responding:

Route 53 → EC2-A
             ↓
        No response ❌
             ↓
        UNHEALTHY

Then, depending on the routing policy, Route 53 can stop returning that unhealthy endpoint and direct users to a healthy one.

User
 ↓
Route 53
 ↓
EC2-A ❌  ← unhealthy, avoid
 ↓
EC2-B ✅  ← healthy, use
Very important

Route 53 doesn't fix the unhealthy EC2 server.

It only checks its health and makes DNS routing decisions based on the result.

Interview answer:
"Route 53 health checks periodically check whether an endpoint is healthy. If an endpoint becomes unhealthy, 
Route 53 can stop routing DNS traffic to it and route users to a healthy endpoint, depending on the routing policy."

1. Hosted Zone — "Where is my domain?"

A Hosted Zone contains the DNS records for your domain.

Example:

myapp.com
   ↓
Hosted Zone
   ↓
Records
   ├── www → Load Balancer
   ├── api → EC2 / Load Balancer
   └── mail → Mail server

So:

Hosted Zone = place where you manage DNS records for a domain.

2. Health Check — "Is my server working?"

Route 53 can check whether your application/server is healthy.

Route 53 Health Check
        ↓
    Application
        ↓
   Healthy? ✅ / ❌

For example, Route 53 can check:

https://myapp.com/health

If it gets a successful response → Healthy.

If the endpoint fails → Unhealthy.

Health Check = checks whether your endpoint is available.

ALB health check → chooses healthy EC2.
Route 53 health check → helps choose a healthy destination such as an ALB/endpoint.

1. Load Balancer Health Check

This checks the EC2 instances behind the Load Balancer.

             ALB
              |
       Health Check
        /          \
     EC2-A        EC2-B
      ✅            ❌
    Healthy      Unhealthy

If EC2-B is unhealthy, the ALB stops sending traffic to EC2-B.

So:

ALB health check = "Which backend EC2 should I send traffic to?"

2. Route 53 Health Check

This checks the endpoint/application itself.

User
 ↓
Route 53
 ↓
Health Check
 ↓
Application / Endpoint

It can be used with Route 53 routing policies.

For example, you have two ALBs:

                 Route 53
                /         \
             ALB-1       ALB-2
               ↓            ↓
             EC2s         EC2s

If ALB-1/application becomes unhealthy:

Route 53
    ↓
ALB-1 ❌
    ↓
ALB-2 ✅

Route 53 can direct DNS traffic to ALB-2 when configured for failover/health-based routing.
The easiest way to remember
Health Check	Checks	Purpose
Load Balancer health check	EC2 targets	Decide which EC2 receives traffic
Route 53 health check	Endpoint/application	Decide which DNS destination should receive traffic

