US ALB

So there are two different ideas:

Failover routing → primary Region fails → use backup Region.
Latency-based routing → send users to the Region that gives them the lowest network latency.

And Route 53 is what makes the DNS decision; the ALBs themselves remain separate regional load balancers.


Remember
2 AZs in ONE ALB
       ↓
ALB health check
       ↓
Checks EC2 targets
2 DIFFERENT ALBs / Regions
       ↓
Route 53 health check
       ↓
Helps Route 53 choose destination







3. Registered Domain — "Who owns the domain?"

Suppose you want:

myapp.com

You need to register that domain.

Route 53 can act as a domain registrar, where you register/manage the domain.

Registered Domain = the domain name you own/manage.

How they work together

Suppose you have:

Domain:
myapp.com
       ↓
Registered Domain
       ↓
Hosted Zone
       ↓
DNS Records
       ↓
Load Balancer
       ↓
EC2

And you can add:

Health Check
       ↓
Checks Load Balancer / application
Easy way to remember
Route 53 feature	Meaning
Registered Domain	Your domain name
Hosted Zone	DNS records for that domain
Health Check	Checks whether an endpoint is healthy

Interview shortcut:

Registered domain = name, Hosted Zone = DNS records, Health Check = server health.


Registered Domain → the domain name you registered, e.g. mycompany.com
Hosted Zone → where you configure DNS records for that domain
Health Check → checks whether your application endpoint is healthy.

In Route 53 Hosted Zone, you create DNS records. Each record tells Route 53 where or how a domain should resolve.

The main record types you should know for interviews are:

Record	Meaning	Example
A	Domain → IPv4 address	example.com → 13.234.50.10
AAAA	Domain → IPv6 address	example.com → IPv6 address
CNAME	Domain → another domain name	www.example.com → example.com
MX	Specifies mail servers	example.com → mail server
TXT	Stores text/verification information	Domain verification, SPF
NS	Specifies authoritative name servers	Hosted zone name servers
SOA	Contains basic DNS/zone information	Zone's authoritative DNS information
Alias	AWS-specific routing to supported AWS resources	example.com → ALB

Easy interview memory

A = IPv4
AAAA = IPv6
CNAME = another domain
MX = email
TXT = text/verification
NS = name servers
SOA = zone information
Alias = AWS resource

-------------------------
What is Alias in Route 53?

Alias is an AWS-specific DNS feature that lets you point your domain directly to a supported AWS resource.
We use Alias mainly when we want our domain to point directly to an AWS resource, especially when using the root domain.
Remember this for interview
Alias is used in Route 53 to point a domain directly to supported AWS resources, such as an ALB or CloudFront,
without manually managing their IP addresses. It is especially useful for the root domain because CNAME cannot be used at the root.
CNAME = point a subdomain to another DNS name.
Alias = point a Route 53 record directly to a supported AWS resource, including when using the root domain.
For example, your ALB has an AWS DNS name:

my-alb-123.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com

Instead of manually managing the ALB's IP addresses, you can do:

hi.com
   ↓
Route 53
   ↓
A Record + Alias ON
   ↓
Application Load Balancer
   ↓
EC2

without Alias, you can use the ALB's DNS name with CNAME, but not for the root domain.
Example:

www.myapp.com
      ↓
CNAME
      ↓
my-alb-123.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com

Here:

www.myapp.com → subdomain ✅
myapp.com → root/apex domain ❌ with CNAME

For the root domain, use Alias:

myapp.com
   ↓
A record + Alias
   ↓
ALB







Why Alias instead of CNAME?
For the root domain:
myapp.com
you generally use A Alias → ALB.
For a subdomain:
www.myapp.com
you can use either A Alias → ALB or a CNAME → ALB DNS name.
So the important idea is:
ALB gives you an AWS DNS name. Route 53 lets your own domain name point to that ALB.



Alias can point to particular supported AWS resources, not just a Load Balancer.
What about EC2?
For a normal EC2 instance, you generally don't use an Alias record directly to the EC2 instance.
So when I said "Alias = AWS resource", I meant supported AWS resources, not every AWS resource.
Easy interview answer:
"A Route 53 Alias record can point a domain to supported AWS resources such as an ALB, NLB, CloudFront distribution, or S3 website endpoint."
When Alias is ON, CNAME is not used. That's because an Alias record is used with certain record types, typically A or AAAA.
CNAME works for a subdomain.


What is happening here?

You are saying to Route 53:

mydomain.com
      ↓
A Record
      ↓
192.0.2.235

192.0.2.235 is an IPv4 address.

So:

Alias OFF → you manually provide an IP address.

If you turn Alias ON

The screen changes. Instead of entering an IP address like:

192.0.2.235

you can select a supported AWS resource, for example:

Alias: ON ✅

Route traffic to:
Application Load Balancer
        ↓
my-alb-xxxxx.elb.amazonaws.com

Then the architecture is:

User
 ↓
www.myapp.com
 ↓
Route 53
 ↓
A Record + Alias
 ↓
ALB
 ↓
EC2



Your screenshot shows:

Record type: CNAME
Alias: OFF
Value: www.example.com

You can put the ALB DNS name in Value:

Record name: www
Record type: CNAME
Alias: OFF

Value:
my-alb-123456.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com

Then:

User
 ↓
www.hi.com
 ↓
Route 53
 ↓
CNAME
 ↓
ALB DNS name
 ↓
ALB
 ↓
EC2
So why use Alias?

Because CNAME has a limitation: you cannot use a CNAME for the root domain.

For example:

www.hi.com  → CNAME → ALB DNS name ✅

But:

hi.com → CNAME → ALB DNS name ❌

For the root domain, use:

hi.com
 ↓
A record + Alias ON
 ↓
ALB
